response:
 First, we note that a geometric range is defined by its first term $a$ and common ratio $r$. The $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by $a \cdot r^{n-1}$. In this problem, we are given the 5th and 11th terms as 11 and 5, respectively. That gives us two equations: \[
11 = a r^4
\] \[
5 = a r^{10}
\] Dividing these two equations gives us \[
\frac{5}{11} = r^6
\] Taking the sixth root of both sides gives us the common ratio $r$: \[
r = \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{1/6}
\] Now that we have the common ratio, we can find the first term $a$ by substituting $r$ back into one of the original equations. Using the first equation, we get \[
11 = a \left(\left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{1/6}\right)^4 = a \left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{2/3}
\] Solving for $a$ gives us \[
a = 11 \left(\frac{11}{5}\right)^{2/3} = 11 \cdot \frac{11^{2/3}}{5^{2/3}} = 11 \cdot \frac{\sqrt[3]{121}}{\sqrt[3]{25}} = \frac{11 \sqrt[3]{121}}{\sqrt[3]{25}}
\] The 8th term of the sequence is given by $a r^7$: \[
\frac{11 \sqrt[3]{121}}{\sqrt[3]{25}} \left(\left(\frac{5}{11}\right)^{1/6}\right)^7 = \frac{11 \sqrt[3]{121}}{\sqrt[3]{25}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt[3]{5^7}}{\sqrt[3]{11^7}} = \frac{11 \cdot \sqrt[3]{121 \cdot 5^7}}{11^2 \cdot \sqrt[3]{25}} = \frac{\sqrt[3]{121 \cdot 5^7}}{11 \cdot \sqrt[3]{25}} = \frac{\sqrt[3]{121 \cdot 5^6 \cdot 5}}{11 \cdot \sqrt[3]{25}} = \frac{5 \cdot \sqrt[3]{121 \cdot 5}}{11 \cdot \sqrt[3]{25}} = \frac{5 \cdot \sqrt[3]{605}}{11 \cdot \sqrt[3]{25}} = \frac{5 \cdot \sqrt[3]{11^2 \cdot 5}}{11 \cdot \sqrt[3]{25}} = \frac{5 \cdot 11 \cdot \sqrt[3]{5}}{11 \cdot \sqrt[3]{25}} = \sqrt[3]{5}
\] <answer> $\sqrt[3]{5}$
</answer>

